---
description: OpenSpec workflow rules for safe AI-assisted development
alwaysApply: false
---

When the user asks to work with OpenSpec, proposals, specs, tests, TDD, Postman, API contracts, webhooks, state transitions, apply, or validation:

1. Do not implement immediately unless the user explicitly asks for apply/implementation.
2. First analyze the existing codebase and project rules.
3. Identify the source of truth in code, configuration, tests, documentation, API contracts, logs, or existing OpenSpec artifacts.
4. Do not invent business rules, class names, endpoints, labels, statuses, response codes, or UI text.
5. Mark unsupported claims as `NOT VERIFIABLE`.
6. Separate what the code guarantees, what the tests verify, what commands/logs show, and what remains unproven.
7. Create or update an OpenSpec proposal before changing product code when the task is new or ambiguous.
8. Define acceptance criteria before implementation.
9. Prefer small, reviewable tasks.
10. Do not change tests only to make them pass.
11. Do not relax assertions without explicit justification.
12. Every test must fail for a meaningful regression.

## Apply And Validation Must Not Hide Partial State

For `/opsx-apply`, `/aplica-tarea`, `/verifica-tarea`, `openspec instructions apply`, or any OpenSpec validation/apply request:

- Follow `.cursor/skills/multiagente/SKILL.md` when present.
- Use `openspec status --change "<slug>" --json` and `openspec instructions apply --change "<slug>" --json` when available.
- Read all OpenSpec context files before changing implementation.
- Work `tasks.md` in order and update checkboxes only after matching verification.
- Run `openspec validate "<slug>"` and relevant project checks before closing when available.
- If the work cannot be completed, final output must include the exact unfinished tasks, why they remain, and the smallest next action.
- Never end an apply/verify turn with an implied success message when any task, gate, or user-visible behavior remains unverified.
